why is the ruble collapsing and what  does it mean basically two explanations  one of them is falling oil prices and  the other is the sanctions about 50  percent of Russia's revenues come from  oil and gas so with the price of a  barrel of oil collapsing from 110  dollars to $60 that's a huge hit to the  country's revenues the second thing is  the sanctions you remember back in  February Russia invaded Crimea the  Russian flag was hoisted above the  Crimean Parliament while President Obama  and other Western leaders responded by  imposing sanctions on the Russian  government and Russian companies we're  making it clear that there are  consequences for their actions  the central bank essentially has two  tools at its disposal one it could dip  into its massive pile of foreign cash  and buy Russian rubles to prompt them up  however they've already done this a few  times and it hasn't worked very well  the second thing Russia can do is  dramatically hike interest rates and  that's exactly what they did early  Tuesday morning the basic idea is if you  increase interest rates you increase the  incentive people have to keep their  money in rubles that works for all of  about 20 minutes after that the Russian  ruble continued its violent downward  spiral losing about 19 percent of its  value in 24 hours essentially fear has  completely taken over the Russian market  as people race for the exits so what's  going to happen the collapsing ruble  will cause inflation to get worse and  the interest rate hikes which they need  to stem the rubles decline will only  deepen the recession  the one way Putin could help himself is  by dialing back the aggression in  Ukraine and hoping the sanctions get  eased but that seems very unlikely the  provocations in Ukraine are the one  thing he's done this year that's been  very popular in Russia it's much easier  for Putin to blame the West for Russia's  problems than to actually do anything  himself
 

